[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Optimistic vs. Pessimistic Concurrency Control in SQL Tuning"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Concurrency control is a critical aspect of database management and SQL tuning. When multiple transactions attempt to read and write data simultaneously, the database engine must manage access to prevent data anomalies while maximizing throughput."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Pessimistic Concurrency Control (PCC)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pessimistic Concurrency Control assumes that conflicts between transactions are likely to happen. Therefore, it locks data resources as soon as they are read or modified, preventing other transactions from accessing them until the current transaction releases the lock."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN TRANSACTION;
SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE account_id = 101 FOR UPDATE;
-- Perform business logic and updates
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE account_id = 101;
COMMIT;"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Pros and Cons of Pessimistic Locking"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Guarantees strict data consistency and prevents lost updates by isolating transactions immediately."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Reduces concurrency, increases latency, and can lead to deadlocks and contention bottlenecks under high-load scenarios."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Optimistic Concurrency Control assumes that data conflicts are rare. Transactions read data without acquiring locks, perform their calculations, and only check for conflicts at the commit phase by validating version numbers or timestamps."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"-- Read data along with version
SELECT balance, version FROM accounts WHERE account_id = 101;

-- At commit time, verify version has not changed
UPDATE accounts 
SET balance = balance - 100, version = version + 1 
WHERE account_id = 101 AND version = 5;"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Pros and Cons of Optimistic Concurrency Control"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Maximizes throughput, eliminates lock overhead, and avoids deadlocks since resources are never locked during read phases."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: High rollback/retry overhead if conflicts frequently occur, leading to wasted CPU cycles when multiple transactions try to update the exact same row."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"SQL Tuning Guidelines and Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Pessimistic Locking (FOR UPDATE) when contention is high and the cost of rolling back a transaction is greater than the cost of waiting for a lock."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Optimistic Concurrency Control in read-heavy environments, web applications, or microservices where user interaction spans long periods and direct database locks would degrade scalability."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always ensure proper indexing on columns used in concurrency checks (like version or timestamp columns) to prevent full table scans during validation steps."}]
